      "explanation": "<img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0px auto;max-height:40vh\" alt=\"JEE Main 2023 (Online) 13th April Evening Shift Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 108 English Explanation\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/1lgsm006a/ddbebe6c-5a07-4b21-b13c-30c1e7d3bc39/0f2e1c20-e165-11ed-95df-fb857d00bffe/file-1lgsm006b.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"><br><b>Statement I:</b> Both SO₂ and H₂O have a V-shaped (or bent) molecular structure. SO₂ has a central sulfur atom with two oxygen atoms and one lone pair, while H₂O has a central oxygen atom with two hydrogen atoms and two lone pairs. In both cases, the repulsion between the lone pairs and the bonded electron pairs leads to a V-shaped structure.\n<br><br>\n<b>Statement II:</b> The bond angle in H₂O is approximately 104.5° due to the tetrahedral arrangement of electron pairs (including the lone pairs) around the central oxygen atom. In SO₂, the bond angle is approximately 119°. The presence of a lone pair and the double bonds in SO₂ results in a different electron distribution, leading to a larger bond angle compared to H₂O."
